 ],
 "literal": "Oh mother, this man is a young student! / In the common ground of his own town, what is he, I wonder? / With a body not filled by begged food, / he crawls in like a guest, this great proud man!",
 "translation": "Oh mother, this man is like a young student! I wonder what he is like in the assembly ground of his own town. With a body unsatisfied by the food he begged, he creeps in like a guest, this great, proud lord!",
 "modern": "அன்னையே! இவன் ஒரு இளைய மாணவன் போல இருக்கிறான். தன் ஊரின் பொதுமன்றத்தில் இவன் எப்படிப்பட்டவனாக இருப்பானோ? இரந்து உண்ட உணவினால் இன்னும் நிறையாத உடம்போடு, புதிய விருந்தினனைப் போல மெல்ல நுழைந்து வருகிறான், இந்தத் தற்பெருமை உடைய தலைவன்!",
 "explanation": "In this Marutam poem, the heroine speaks mockingly to her foster-mother or friend about her unfaithful husband who has returned from his courtesan. To appease her anger and gain entry into the house, he humbles himself, acting submissive like a young, poor student begging for food. The heroine sarcastically wonders how such a great, proud lord behaves in his own town's public assembly, contrasting it with his current pathetic, crawling posture. This imagery perfectly fits the Marutam landscape, which deals with infidelity, sulking, and the husband's deceptive attempts at reconciliation. Key words like 'iḷamāṇākkaṉ' (young student) and 'cemmalaṉ' (proud lord) highlight the sharp irony of his behavior.",
